497	
498	  <div class="era-card">
499	    <h4>Golden Age (1934–1959)</h4>
500	    <div class="era-stats">
501	      <div>Ceremonies: <span>26</span></div>
502	      <div>Unique Composers: <span>25</span></div>
503	      <div>Top Composer: <span>Dimitri Tiomkin (3 wins)</span></div>
504	      <div>Repeat Rate: <span>3.8%</span></div>
505	    </div>
506	    <p>Dominated by European emigre composers who brought late-Romantic orchestral traditions to Hollywood. Studio system composers like Max Steiner, Alfred Newman, and Dimitri Tiomkin defined a lush, leitmotif-driven style. Repeat winners were common — a handful of titans collected the bulk of awards.</p>
507	  </div>
508	
509	  <div class="era-card">
510	    <h4>Silver Age (1960–1979)</h4>
511	    <div class="era-stats">
512	      <div>Ceremonies: <span>20</span></div>
513	      <div>Unique Composers: <span>18</span></div>
514	      <div>Top Composer: <span>Nino Rota (2 wins)</span></div>
515	      <div>Repeat Rate: <span>10.0%</span></div>
516	    </div>
517	    <p>A transitional period as the studio system dissolved and new voices emerged. Maurice Jarre's epic canvases, Henry Mancini's jazz-inflected melodies, and John Barry's sophisticated themes signaled diversification. The era closed with John Williams' Star Wars, a bridge to blockbuster scoring.</p>
518	  </div>
519	
520	  <div class="era-card">
521	    <h4>Modern Era (1980–1999)</h4>
522	    <div class="era-stats">
523	      <div>Ceremonies: <span>20</span></div>
524	      <div>Unique Composers: <span>18</span></div>
525	      <div>Top Composer: <span>Alan Menken (3 wins)</span></div>
526	      <div>Repeat Rate: <span>10.0%</span></div>
527	    </div>
528	    <p>The blockbuster age. John Williams and Alan Menken dominated with a combined 7 wins across adventure, sci-fi, and Disney animation. Electronic and world-music influences entered via Vangelis, Herbie Hancock, and Tan Dun. The composer pool broadened significantly.</p>
529	  </div>
530	
531	  <div class="era-card">
532	    <h4>Contemporary Era (2000–2026)</h4>
533	    <div class="era-stats">
534	      <div>Ceremonies: <span>27</span></div>
535	      <div>Unique Composers: <span>22</span></div>
536	      <div>Top Composer: <span>Ludwig Göransson (4 wins)</span></div>
537	      <div>Repeat Rate: <span>18.5%</span></div>
538	    </div>
539	    <p>The most diverse era in Oscar scoring history. No single composer dominates. International voices (Tan Dun, A.R. Rahman, Gustavo Santaolalla), genre-bending artists (Trent Reznor & Atticus Ross), and global stars (Hans Zimmer, Alexandre Desplat) reflect the Academy's expanding sensibility. Ludwig Göransson's three wins across two decades underscore a new model of versatile, genre-fluid composition.</p>
540	  </div>
541	
542	  <div class="narrative">
543	    The four-era framework reveals the <strong>central story of this dataset: a progressive dispersal of dominance</strong>. In the Golden Age, <strong>25 composers</strong> shared 26 awards, with a repeat rate of 3.8%. By the Contemporary Era, <strong>22 composers</strong> share the same number of ceremonies, and the repeat rate has dropped to 18.5%. The trend is unmistakable — each era is more competitive and more diverse than the last.
544	  </div>
