551	    <h2 class="section-title">Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>
552	  </div>
553	  <ul class="milestones">
554	    <li><strong>1934</strong> — The inaugural Best Original Score Oscar goes to Louis Silvers for <em>One Night of Love</em>.</li>
555	<li><strong>1943</strong> — Max Steiner becomes the first repeat winner, taking his second award for <em>Now, Voyager</em>.</li>
556	<li><strong>All-time leaders</strong> — John Williams holds the record with 4 wins across the full history of the award.</li>
557	<li><strong>Longest gap between wins</strong> — Hans Zimmer waited 27 years between wins (1994 to 2021).</li>
558	<li><strong>2019</strong> — Hildur Guðnadóttir becomes the first woman to win Best Original Score for <em>Joker</em>.</li>
559	<li><strong>2026</strong> — Ludwig Göransson wins for <em>Sinners</em>, marking his third historic victory and the most recent entry in the dataset.</li>
560	<li><strong>1981</strong> — Vangelis wins for <em>Chariots of Fire</em>, the first predominantly synthesizer-based score to take the award.</li>
561	<li><strong>1989–1992</strong> — Alan Menken wins three times in four years for Disney Renaissance films (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>).</li>
562	
563	  </ul>
