567	<div class="section">
568	  <div class="section-header">
569	    <div class="section-number">6</div>
570	    <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
571	  </div>
572	  <div class="conclusions">
573	    
574	<ol>
575	    <li><strong>Concentration has given way to diversity.</strong> In the Golden Age, a small cadre of emigre composers collected the majority of awards. By the Contemporary Era, the winner pool has expanded dramatically — the 2020s alone have produced eight unique winners across eight ceremonies, with no repeat.</li>
576	    <li><strong>The blockbuster era transformed the award.</strong> The emergence of John Williams and Alan Menken in the 1980s–90s created a new template: the composer as franchise architect. Their combined 7 wins in the Modern Era account for 35% of that era's ceremonies.</li>
577	    <li><strong>Internationalization is accelerating.</strong> Winners since 2000 hail from China, India, Argentina, Italy, Iceland, Germany, and Sweden. The award has moved decisively beyond the Hollywood studio system that birthed it.</li>
578	    <li><strong>Genre boundaries have dissolved.</strong> Electronic musicians (Trent Reznor, Atticus Ross), jazz artists (Herbie Hancock), and classically trained composers now compete on equal footing — a shift that would have been unthinkable in the orchestral-mandatory Golden Age.</li>
579	    <li><strong>Repeat dominance is rarer but still possible.</strong> Ludwig Göransson's three wins (2018, 2023/2024, 2026) demonstrate that the Academy still rewards sustained excellence, but the longer intervals between wins and the diversity of intervening winners suggest a more competitive landscape than any previous era.</li>
580	</ol>
581	
582	  </div>
583	  <div class="narrative" style="margin-top:20px;">
584	    As the Academy Award for Best Original Score approaches its centennial in the 2030s, the category stands at an inflection point. The convergence of orchestral tradition, electronic innovation, and global voices suggests that the next decade will continue to reward versatility and originality over any single compositional school. The data points to an increasingly level playing field — one where first-time nominees and established figures compete on equal footing, and where the definition of a "film score" continues to expand.
585	  </div>
