ed {longest_gap['gap']} years between wins ({longest_gap['from_year']} to {longest_gap['to_year']}).</li>\n"", "+milestones_html += "<li><strong>2019</strong> — Hildur Guðnadóttir becomes the first woman to win Best Original Score for <em>Joker</em>.</li>\n"", "+milestones_html += "<li><strong>2026</strong> — Ludwig Göransson wins for <em>Sinners</em>, his most recent victory and the latest entry in the dataset.</li>\n"", "+milestones_html += "<li><strong>1981</strong> — Vangelis wins for <em>Chariots of Fire</em>, the first predominantly synthesizer-based score to take the award.</li>\n"", "+milestones_html += "<li><strong>1989–1992</strong> — Alan Menken wins three times in four years for Disney Renaissance films (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>).</li>\n"", "+milestones_html += "<li><strong>1986</strong> — Herbie Hancock wins for <em>Round Midnight</em>, marking jazz\'s first recognition in the category.</li>\n"", " ", "-# First woman", "-women_composers = [c for c in df_ind['Composer'].unique() if any(kw in c.lower() for kw in ['hildur'])]", "-if women_composers:", "-    wc = women_composers[0]", "-    wc_year = df_ind[df_ind['Composer'] == wc]['Year'].min()", "-    wc_film = df[df['Year'] == wc_year]['Film'].values[0]", "-    milestones_html += f"<li><strong>{wc_year}</strong> — {wc} becomes the first woman to win Best Original Score for <em>{wc_film}</em>.</li>\n"", "-", "-# Most recent", "-milestones_html += f"<li><strong>2026</strong> — Ludwig Göransson wins for <em>Sinners</em>, marking his third historic victory and the most recent entry in the dataset.</li>\n"", "-", "-# First electronic", "-milestones_html += f"<li><strong>1981</strong> — Vangelis wins for <em>Chariots of Fire</em>, the first predominantly synthesizer-based score to take the award.</li>\n"", "-", "-# Disney animation dominance", "-milestones_html += f"<li><strong>1989–1992</strong> — Alan Menken wins three times in four years for Disney Renaissance films (<em>The Little Mermaid</em>, <em>Beauty and the Beast</em>, <em>Aladdin</em>).</li>\n"", "-", " # Conclusions", " conclusions_html = """", " <ol>", "-    <li><strong>Concentration has given way to diversity.</strong> In the Golden Age, a small cadre of emigre composers collected the majority of awards. By the Contemporary Era, the winner pool has expanded dramatically — the 2020s alone have produced eight unique winners across eight ceremonies, with no repeat.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Concentration has given way to diversity.</strong> In the Golden Age, a small cadre of emigre composers collected the majority of awards. By the Contemporary Era, the winner pool has expanded dramatically — the 2020s have produced 7 unique winners across 7 ceremonies, with zero repeat winners.</li>", "     <li><strong>The blockbuster era transformed the award.</strong> The emergence of John Williams and Alan Menken in the 1980s–90s created a new template: the composer as franchise architect. Their combined 7 wins in the Modern Era account for 35% of that era's ceremonies.</li>", "     <li><strong>Internationalization is accelerating.</strong> Winners since 2000 hail from China, India, Argentina, Italy, Iceland, Germany, and Sweden. The award has moved decisively beyond the Hollywood studio system that birthed it.</li>", "     <li><strong>Genre boundaries have dissolved.</strong> Electronic musicians (Trent Reznor, Atticus Ross), jazz artists (Herbie Hancock), and classically trained composers now compete on equal footing — a shift that would have been unthinkable in the orchestral-mandatory Golden Age.</li>", "-    <li><strong>Repeat dominance is rarer but still possible.</strong> Ludwig Göransson's three wins (2018, 2023/2024, 2026) demonstrate that the Academy still rewards sustained excellence, but the longer intervals between wins and the diversity of intervening winners suggest a more competitive landscape than any previous era.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Repeat dominanc